Next RIG workspace pass: implement ACTIVE PART SELECTION + COMPACT OUTLINER +
SELECTED-PART INSPECTOR.

Do NOT implement viewport picking, rotation gizmos, drag-to-retime, bake-to-keys,
or generator changes in this build.

This pass is deliberately one coherent structural change.

==================================================
WHY THIS IS FIRST
==================================================

The current RIG sidebar renders the full controls for EVERY rig part.

That scales badly:

    6-part preset  -> barely manageable
    11-part rig    -> wall of controls
    larger rig     -> unusable

Blender's important pattern is not its styling.

It is:

    SELECT SOMETHING
          ->
    EDIT THE ACTIVE THING

We need that model first.

Everything later:

    viewport picking
    pose-then-key
    gizmos
    dope-sheet selection
    transform inspector

depends on having a canonical active part.

==================================================
IMPORTANT: rigPickIdx IS NOT SELECTION
==================================================

Do NOT reuse:

    rigPickIdx

as the selected body part.

In the current code rigPickIdx is assignment/picking state:

    const isPicking = rigPickIdx === i

and it is cleared after model assignment:

    setRigPickIdx(null)

That is a temporary workflow flag, not persistent object selection.

Introduce:

    const [rigSelectedIdx, setRigSelectedIdx] = React.useState(...)

Selection and model assignment must be independent.

Eventually rigPickIdx may become unnecessary, but do not remove it unless its
current assignment workflow is fully replaced and verified.

==================================================
1. ADD A CANONICAL ACTIVE PART
==================================================

Introduce:

    rigSelectedIdx

Rules:

    no parts:
        selected = null

    preset/load creates parts:
        select part 0 by default

    ADD PART:
        select newly-added part

    delete selected:
        select nearest surviving part

    click another part:
        selection changes

    changing ACTION:
        selection remains

    scrubbing timeline:
        selection remains

    playing/stopping:
        selection remains

Do not couple selection to:

    forgePickFile
    rigPickIdx
    rigActionIdx
    rigFrame

==================================================
2. TURN BODY PARTS INTO A COMPACT OUTLINER
==================================================

Replace the current full editor repeated once per part with a compact list.

Each row should show roughly:

    [index]  [indent] PART NAME       [model status] [key indicator]

Example:

    1  TORSO             ●
      2  HEAD            ◆
      3  ARM L           ◆
      4  ARM R           ◆
      5  LEG L
      6  LEG R

Use parentIdx to indent children.

Do NOT build a sophisticated collapsible tree yet.

A simple visual hierarchy is enough:

    depth = number of parents
    paddingLeft = depth * small amount

Keep the existing rule:

    parent must refer to an earlier part

No hierarchy semantics change.

==================================================
3. KEY INDICATOR
==================================================

For each part row, inspect the SELECTED action.

If its track contains keys:

    show ◆

If it has procedural animation but no keys:

    show a small motion indicator such as ~

If static:

    no indicator

This makes the outliner useful rather than merely shorter.

Do not make these icons editable in this pass.

==================================================
4. CLICKING A ROW SELECTS
==================================================

The entire row should select:

    onClick={() => setRigSelectedIdx(i)}

Selected row receives the existing Forge/RIG accent treatment.

Do not overload clicking the row with:

    assigning models
    deleting
    changing parent

Those belong in the inspector.

The user should be able to click rapidly through the hierarchy without
accidentally editing it.

==================================================
5. REMOVE FULL CONTROLS FROM EVERY ROW
==================================================

This is the important cleanup.

Do NOT continue rendering:

    model assignment
    parent dropdown
    offsets
    scale
    procedural axis controls
    keyframe fields

inside every part row.

Render those ONCE, below the outliner, for:

    rigParts[rigSelectedIdx]

This is the selected-part inspector.

==================================================
6. SPLIT THE INSPECTOR INTO TWO REGIONS
==================================================

Inside the SAME 240px right sidebar:

    TRANSFORM
    ANIMATION

Do not create another sidebar.

Do not change overall Forge panel width.

--------------------------------
TRANSFORM
--------------------------------

Contains structure only:

    PART NAME / LABEL
    MODEL
    ASSIGN / CLEAR MODEL
    PARENT
    OFFSET X
    OFFSET Y
    OFFSET Z
    SCALE
    DELETE PART

These values describe what the skeleton IS.

--------------------------------
ANIMATION
--------------------------------

Contains motion only for the selected ACTION:

    mode: PROCEDURAL / KEYS

If PROCEDURAL:

    X enabled
      amplitude
      speed
      phase

    Y enabled
      amplitude
      speed
      phase

    Z enabled
      amplitude
      speed
      phase

If KEYS:

    CURRENT FRAME
    X
    Y
    Z
    KEY
    CLEAR KEY

Use the existing motionOf / setRigActions / setKeyAt / clearKeyAt logic.

Do NOT redesign the clip data model.

==================================================
7. MODELS SHOULD BECOME PART OF TRANSFORM WORKFLOW
==================================================

The existing MODELS section currently consumes a large standalone region.

For this pass, keep the available-model picker if necessary, but make the
workflow clearly target the ACTIVE part.

Preferred behaviour:

    select ARM L
    click/import/select model
    ASSIGN TO ARM L

The UI should visibly say which part receives the model.

Example:

    MODEL
    [ BARON_ARM.MOD ▼ ]
    [ ASSIGN ]

Do not require scrolling down to locate the matching part afterward.

If moving the entire MODELS chooser into the selected inspector is too invasive,
leave the chooser where it is but make assignment target rigSelectedIdx.

Do not combine this with file-import architecture changes.

==================================================
8. PRESETS ARE GLOBAL, NOT PART PROPERTIES
==================================================

PRESETS should remain outside the selected-part inspector.

They replace/create an entire skeleton.

They are not a property of TORSO or ARM L.

Keep them as a compact global section.

Likewise:

    CAMERA
    ACTIONS

are rig/global state.

Conceptual sidebar order:

    CAMERA
    PRESETS
    ACTIONS

    BODY PARTS
      compact outliner

    TRANSFORM
      selected part

    ANIMATION
      selected part / selected action

This already removes most of the current confusion.

==================================================
9. DOPE SHEET SHOULD FOLLOW SELECTION
==================================================

Do not redesign the dope sheet.

But make the selected part visually clear there.

The existing dope sheet already has:

    SUMMARY row
    one row per part
    diamonds
    playhead

Add selected-row emphasis:

    selected outliner part
        ==
    highlighted dope-sheet row

Clicking a dope-sheet part label may also select that part.

Do NOT implement diamond dragging yet.

Do NOT alter key times in this pass.

==================================================
10. SELECTION SHOULD EVENTUALLY BE SHARED WITH VIEWPORT
==================================================

Design rigSelectedIdx as the canonical state that future viewport picking will
write to.

Future:

    click mesh
       ->
    setRigSelectedIdx(partIdx)

For now DO NOT add raycasting.

The outliner is the only selection source needed for this build.

This lets us establish all downstream UI semantics before adding Three.js
picking complexity.

==================================================
11. DO NOT ADD A ROTATION GIZMO YET
==================================================

Pose-then-key is absolutely the desired next-generation workflow.

But it requires:

    selectable viewport objects
    reliable partIdx -> THREE.Group identity
    raycasting
    selection highlighting
    TransformControls or equivalent
    local-vs-world rotation semantics
    temporary pose state
    writing pose to a key
    orbit/gizmo pointer conflict handling
    shared viewport regression testing

That is its own feature.

Do not smuggle half of it into this selection cleanup.

==================================================
12. NO GENERATOR CHANGES
==================================================

Do not touch:

    generateRigBAS
    procedural output
    keyframe BAS output
    MODLOAD
    MODSET
    MODJOIN
    MODPOS
    lossless migration gate

This pass changes editor presentation/state only.

The same rigActions and rigParts must reach the generator afterward.

==================================================
13. NO DATA MODEL MIGRATION
==================================================

Do not add fields to persisted rig parts merely for selection.

rigSelectedIdx is editor state.

It does not need to exist in:

    skeleton
    actions
    export
    RIG.BAS

If selection is saved at all, keep it as workspace/UI state, not rig semantics.

